Reason:
The bug occurs because the code attempts to manage mouse transparency on a per-node basis, specifically for `previousNode`. This leads to unintended interactions during transitions, as `previousNode` can still process mouse events when it should not, due to the incorrect handling of its mouse transparency state.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```java
view.setMouseTransparent(false);
view.setMouseTransparent(true);
```
Reason:
The fix involves setting the mouse transparency for the entire view instead of individual nodes. By setting `view.setMouseTransparent(true)`, it ensures that all mouse events are ignored during the transition, preventing any unintended interactions with `previousNode`. The subsequent call to `view.setMouseTransparent(false)` allows mouse events to be processed again after the transition is complete.

